**Rule (Ownership, replay and refusal)** · `rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-lc-omega-lifecycle`

Retain one shared bindings declaration owner and one frozen `Rc<Cas>` for the whole correspondence, with one checked receipt, rather than one question/CAS closure per positional row. Existing question and symbol owners are shared by edges, never deep-copied; distinct graph allocations are counted once each. The receiving law graph is built in an exclusively owned mutable `Cas` during preparation, with `Limits` derived from the receiving-policy meet defined above: every original source and mapped symbol-store policy together with the caller's cumulative ledger, never default limits or a reset allowance. Construct or replay there, complete all fallible correspondence checks, close the transaction and then move that exact store into `Rc<Cas>` as part of sealing. The receipt's owner identity must survive that move. The caller keeps no mutable store borrow, checkpoint or rollback capability after sealing. The correspondence API exposes no mutation, `Rc::get_mut`, interior-mutability or deep-copy escape. CAS may retain its internal decision-cache `Mutex`; it grants no mutation of the sealed LC judgment through this API. Read access only borrows the frozen owner; budgeted token sharing retains the same declaration allocation.

For `certificate` and `check_declaration`, the caller holds original residence minus the captured owner increment and excludes the correspondence's `retained_bytes()` for the whole call: each method reserves both internally, including the graph through CAS on certificate export. All unrelated owners and additional token slots remain caller-reserved. Resume full original and correspondence residence immediately on return, and reserve any exported certificate before further work. These are sibling reservation handoffs with no uncounted work, as for construction and replay; the returned correspondence is never counted twice.

The separate planned operation is `LcOmegaCorrespondence::authenticate_join(&self, expected: &LcOmegaDeclarationToken, expected_arithmetic: u32, budget: &mut Budget) -> Result<(), AdmissionError>`. It returns no symbols, request, receipt or graph access. Under the same owner-plus-correspondence reservation as `check_declaration`, first charge one Work and call the existing `Receipt::request(&Cas)` to authenticate the frozen store owner, judgment lifetime and checked-evidence edge; then charge the existing one-Work token `check_same(expected)`; finally charge one Work to compare the authenticated request's `arithmetic` with `expected_arithmetic`. Successful added Work is exactly 3; a budget with two remaining spends 2 and refuses the third visit, while a zero allowance spends zero. Matrix supplies its supported exact-arithmetic revision (1); the actual value is read from the sealed CAS request, not from a second matrix constant. A mismatch returns `ValueMismatch`. A foreign receipt failure retains the precise CAS fault; a different declaration retains `ValueMismatch`. The foreign-receipt subcase is crate-internal defensive evidence: public matrix prepare cannot replace the sealed receipt. A caller-supplied wrong expected arithmetic revision is reachable through this bindings operation. The operation allocates nothing, acquires no edge, exports no certificate and performs no replay. Retained/staged allowance is the original owner increment plus B_corr, with no new scratch allocation. All byte reservations restore on either return and completed Work stays spent. Existing `check_declaration` remains the separate one-Work identity-only method with unchanged pins.

Join-time expected-declaration comparison is the exact token identity followed, on the matrix side, by `check_originals` against independently borrowed matrix originals. The CAS request was compared field-for-field with the translation of that token at sealing; the immutable correspondence still holds that token and the same frozen receipt/store. `authenticate_join` re-authenticates that receipt and verifies this exact token, using the sealed relationship rather than reconstructing or retaining a second CAS request. It does not claim to repeat the full expected-request comparison. Thus no new expectation allocation changes B_corr or the 48-byte carrier. This explicit composition satisfies receipt re-authentication and independent declaration expectation without weakening either check. The new method's three-visit and byte boundaries extend `lc_omega_correspondence_resource_boundaries`; its refusal ownership extends `lc_omega_correspondence_failure_ownership`. These method extensions remain planned until implemented.

The receiving store's permanent ceiling is the meet taken at construction, including the caller's then-active transient reservations P, U−424 and the internally reserved 424-byte owner increment and its Work ceiling; subsequent meets only tighten it.

Portable replay uses a separate exclusively owned receiving store and the independently expected receiving declaration, then freezes that store in the same way. It is an explicitly charged construction path, never a fresh ledger or a copy of the source store. The matrix join re-authenticates the sealed receipt against its held frozen owner and compares the exact declaration expectation; it does not execute portable replay again because registered construction already runs the independent checker before publication under (`rule:nrz:cas-lc-root-registration`), and the frozen owner cannot roll that judgment back. CAS guarantees that a stale/rolled-back receipt refuses, as covered by its internal registered-boundaries control; bindings has no public rollback route. A directly supplied foreign-owner receipt still refuses at the bindings seal. This refines the matrix phase inventory under (`dec:nrz:matrix-loans-lc-origin-encoding`); the staged mutable store and frozen owner retain the same graph allocations, not simultaneous graph copies; the new Rc owner/control-block allocation is charged before freezing.

Construction validates the original declaration, prepares its exact CAS request, invokes registered construction and seals only after all correspondence checks succeed. Replay takes an independently expected receiving declaration/token and its original translation, invokes registered receiving-store replay and seals a fresh receiving correspondence. Admission of that independently supplied receiving token is replay's recheck of the physical and symbol correspondence; its Work is a caller prefix, and the correspondence call does not repeat it. Source-process allocation identities are never portable authority. The caller supplies the receiving originals; replay does not reconstruct an expected declaration by trusting the certificate. Exported mathematical certificates contain no private output-symbol capability. A source receipt presented directly to a foreign CAS refuses; portable replay is the explicit alternative.

Bindings admission borrows the expected bindings declaration owner/token; the matrix preparation step borrows the matrix declaration. Failure preserves both original owners, symbol identities and specialization for retry. The future matrix consuming join returns the identical supplied `LcOmegaOutput` on failure, including after partial CAS Work. It may not redeclare symbols, replace the owner or reset the ledger. Capture and validate the owning original inventory before publishing its token; every acquired edge has its release prepaid, including failure cleanup. Stage fallible bindings metadata before sealing; no partial correspondence escapes. If a later bindings check fails after registered CAS publication, the private receiving Cas and its judgment are destroyed; neither a reusable judgment nor a partial correspondence escapes. Do not promise rollback of unrelated completed CAS work; CAS failures themselves retain their registered atomicity guarantee. Numerical storage is not allocated in this phase.

Use the existing refusal vocabulary, preserving cause and location. Every bindings-originated error below reaches matrix as `LcShapeError::Declaration(AdmissionError)` with its cause intact; only a comparison performed by the matrix join itself produces `LoanError::TargetMismatch`.

|Condition|Bindings outcome|Matrix boundary|
|---------|----------------|---------------|
|Foreign or changed question/declaration, ordered input, selected position or translatable constraint|`AdmissionError::ValueMismatch`; a foreign CAS handle instead preserves its `Algebra` fault.|Preserve `LcShapeError::Declaration(..)`; do not collapse the cause.|
|Constraint or coordinate outside the exact translation table (defensive; unreachable from an admitted forward cover)|`AdmissionError::UnsupportedRecipe`; no approximation or dropped premise. Its documentation must cover this closed translation fragment as well as homogeneous coherent differences.|Preserve `LcShapeError::Declaration(..)`.|
|Wrong physical kind, unit or one-radian relation|`ValueMismatch`; descriptor arithmetic failure remains `AdmissionError::Units`.|Preserve `LcShapeError::Declaration(..)`.|
|Wrong requested output value symbol|`AdmissionError::ResultMismatch`; implementation must widen its current copy-source-specific documentation to include a planned result's mismatch with the checked original recipe.|Preserve `LcShapeError::Declaration(..)`.|
|Wrong companion kind, target or scope|`AdmissionError::CompanionMismatch`.|Preserve `LcShapeError::Declaration(..)`.|
|Receipt/certificate of another category or unsupported version|Typed receipt admission excludes other categories; untrusted portable input preserves CAS `UnsupportedCertificate` or `UnsupportedVersion` through `AdmissionError::Algebra`.|Preserve `LcShapeError::Declaration(..)`.|
|Foreign receipt owner, missing or rolled-back judgment|Preserve CAS `Invalid(ForeignHandle)` or the precise invalid-reference fault through `AdmissionError::Algebra`.|Preserve `LcShapeError::Declaration(..)`.|
|CAS Work, byte or permanent-store exhaustion|Preserve the exact CAS resource fault through `AdmissionError::Algebra`; bindings metadata traversal/capacity uses `ResourceLimit` where applicable. Spent Work remains spent.|Preserve `LcShapeError::Declaration(..)`.|
|Join itself finds a wrong token or symbol (crate-internal defensive subcases), or a mismatched tile schema|No bindings error is invented.|`LoanError::TargetMismatch`; a foreign execution account remains `ForeignAccount` before native work. Return the unchanged declaration.|

These are declaration/authority/resource refusals, not cell outcomes Invalid, Undecided or Missing, and not proofs of infeasibility. They do not change the existing `OriginUnavailable` publication refusal.

**Rule (Cumulative correspondence resource boundary)** · `rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-lc-omega-resources`

Charge the sequence on the original CAS ledger: declaration/token authentication and every positional/companion visit; physical-kind and exact-radian checks; original-coordinate/constraint translation and CAS syntax admission; registered construction or replay; final exact expectation comparison and retained correspondence metadata. Owning-originals admission scans prior input values in order and then compares the output against every input: for N=5, 45+10 alias visits. Charge each comparison before use. Charge before each operation, including allocation and owner-edge acquisition; never refund a refused Work prefix or obtain fresh allowance by retry. The implementation must enumerate these events rather than assigning an unmeasured constant to a generic wrapper. Existing declaration costs are included only when the call creates that declaration; an already admitted declaration's earlier Work is a caller prefix, not charged again. Certificate transport copies remain separate, explicitly metered operations.

The matrix phase vocabulary is `W_bind = W_shape + W_original + W_expression + W_root + W_edges`. `W_shape` covers fixed cardinalities, requested specialization, kinds, companions and exact-radian checks; `W_original` covers original question/token authentication, each constraint/positional symbol visit and final exact expectation comparison; `W_expression` covers the translation table's syntax admission; `W_edges` covers token/frozen-owner sealing, allocation bookkeeping and every retaining-edge acquisition and its eventual release. Each event belongs to exactly one partition. Root construction contributes the complete `W_root` once. For the explicit portable path, `W_bind_replay = W_shape_recv + W_original_recv + W_expression_recv + W_replay + W_edges_recv`, using its actual receiving-side visits. A matrix join does not add `W_replay` to either sealed result: the expanded J worksheet in (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-lc-bridge-resources`) includes receipt-owner/liveness and exact-arithmetic authentication, followed by the ordered symbol/source comparisons and funded native capture. On a ledger with prior Work W_0, admission needs W_0 plus the relevant phase; refusal retains its performed prefix. Transport certificate copies are separately metered, never an implicit replay at every join.

The owning-originals boundary pins its incremental allocation over the caller's reservation; the whole-lifecycle union and T_corr come with construction. The caller continues to reserve existing question, relation, radian and symbol-graph residence. No original graph or physical definition is copied or redeclared. The retained increment includes the owner record, its shared control block, captured pair capacity and initial token. Once admission returns, the caller reserves the 424-byte owner increment throughout its residence, including unrelated work and `check_same`, which makes no internal reservation. For direct calls to `share` and `check_originals`, responsibility transfers to their internal reservation; the caller excludes that same increment for the duration of those calls and restores its reservation before any further work. Admission itself reserves its working envelope before an owner exists. This is the owner-handoff reservation model: it preserves the existing owner tariffs (`owner.rs`) and counts each live allocation once. Reservation handoffs permit no intervening work with uncounted residence. Extra already-held token slots belong to caller residence. For correspondence construction and replay, U names the original baseline including the owner increment. The caller reserves U minus that known 424-byte increment for the duration of the call. The correspondence reserves the 424 itself around every phase except its nested `share`, which reserves it internally. Close the correspondence's owner-reservation scope before entering `share` and open a fresh 424-byte scope before the next operation. These are sequential scopes nested under the caller's unchanged U−424 reservation; nothing releases a portion of an active outer scope. At the call boundary the caller resumes reservation of its full original residence before further work. Bindings does not measure U. Sharing reserves a new token slot and prepays its eventual release.

The five-position owner inventory is 424 bytes on the supported target: a 160-byte record (question 8, pair vector 24, positions 16, counts 24, output bundle 24, radian 8, three exact quantity tags 48, operation 1 and padding 7), a 16-byte shared control block, an 8-byte initial token and 240 bytes for five 48-byte pairs. The question edge retains its relation; the radian edge retains its reference. Their existing allocations remain baseline residence rather than new edge-slot allocations. The conservative staged admission envelope is 504 bytes: the retained increment plus the unchanged 80-byte checked borrowed view. Actual pair capacity is rechecked before capture. This is a logical allocation envelope, not a native allocator peak.

The admission inventory is 1017 Work: preflight 1; pair allocation and prepaid cleanup 2; question/radian/output edge acquisitions and releases 10; descriptor/operation tags 4; five accessor visits and sixty symbol acquisition/release visits 65; alias comparisons 55; unchanged borrowed validation 876; shared-owner allocation/cleanup 2; initial token acquisition/release 2. Independent indexed comparison is 40 visits (fixed identity 1, accessors 5, value/companion identities 33, registry relation 1). Token identity costs 1; sharing costs 2 and adds 8 output bytes over 424 retained bytes, giving a 432-byte staged envelope. The exact/one-below controls preserve prior Work 7 and caller reservation 37: admission Work 1024/1023, retained output 424/423 and staged 541/540; comparison Work 47/46 and staged 461/460; sharing Work 9/8, output 8/7 and staged 469/468. These owner controls are separate from the unchanged borrowed-view controls; their evidence is listed below.

Retained and peak bytes have distinct names. Under (`dec:nrz:matrix-loans-lc-origin-encoding`), `B_corr` is only the newly retained correspondence increment, excluding already resident original question/declaration allocations. Its disjoint allocation partition is `H(Correspondence)+B_original+B_expression+B_law+B_expect+B_symbols`. `B_original` counts only newly retained original-owner records/edges absent from baseline; `B_expression` counts new translated graph/header/constraint/target records, excluding the law judgment; `B_law` is the retained registered law and receipt inventory; `B_expect` is additional retained expected-claim/token comparison metadata not already owned by another term, zero where it is borrowed from those owners; `B_symbols` counts newly owned declaration/symbol records only. Existing symbols are shared, not redeclared. These terms count each allocation once. B_root and B_replay name full CAS lifecycle peaks; B_law and B_expect name retained inventories. The implementation must enumerate capacities, control blocks and edges for each term.

Call the whole simultaneous staged bound `T_corr,x`, for x=root or replay. For each phase t, let C_x(t) be the live CAS lifecycle inventory, A_x(t) the bindings inventory and S_x(t) their actually shared allocations; P is unrelated retained caller bytes. Let U be the original question/declaration baseline, including the 424-byte owner increment, excluded from C_x and A_x here. The caller holds U−424 during the call; O_x(t)=424 is reserved by the correspondence in each phase except `share`, where the owner operation reserves it. Then `T_corr,x = P + (U−424) + max_t(O_x(t) + bytes(C_x(t)) + bytes(A_x(t)) - bytes(S_x(t)))`. The combined original residence remains covered throughout and is excluded from the retained increment B_corr; bindings does not compute U's byte measure. During the CAS subcall, its original graphs are counted once and its full lifecycle peak is B_root or B_replay; outside that subcall only still-live CAS allocations remain, not expired scratch. Preflight each staged/permanent peak on the original ceilings before allocation. Do not sum disjoint peaks, count original graphs twice, or confuse logical CAS payload with native heap bytes. Dropping temporary reservations restores bytes, never Work.

The matrix definitions and these partitions govern together. `lc_origin_phase_work_boundaries` must pin W_bind, the explicit portable path and the join without a second replay; `lc_origin_retained_staged_boundaries` must distinguish retained B_corr from T_corr,x, baseline residence and simultaneous replacement peaks. Literal bindings tariffs and inventories remain implementation evidence with nonzero Work and retained-byte prefixes. Current CAS fixture figures are W_root=173,200, B_root=41,675, W_replay=187,375 and B_replay=48,711; they are not assumed to be the new translated fixture's literal costs. Cache-hit shortcuts and cold/warm evidence remain deferred under (`req:nrz:lc-evaluation-cache-evidence`); frozen-owner authentication at a join is not a cache-hit claim.

